mainmenu "nRF9160 agentic pendant"

config PENDANT_RELAY_API_KEY
	string "AI Pendant cloud relay API key (shared admin key - legacy)"
	default ""
	help
	  The SHARED ADMIN key. It authenticates as principal kind 'admin' with
	  scopes ['*'], which means a pendant carrying it can also reach
	  /v1/ops/*, the routine scheduler, and every other device's data. It is
	  byte-identical to RELAY_API_KEY in the repo-root .env, so the copy on
	  this chip is the same secret the Mac and every future node use, and
	  losing the pendant means rotating it everywhere at once.

	  Kept only as the migration fallback. Set PENDANT_RELAY_DEVICE_TOKEN
	  instead and leave this empty once the pendant is paired.

config PENDANT_RELAY_DEVICE_TOKEN
	string "AI Pendant relay device token (scoped, revocable)"
	default ""
	help
	  This pendant's own credential, of the form pdt_<id>.<secret>, minted
	  once by:

	    node software/ai-pendant-simulator/scripts/pendant-credentials.mjs \
	      pair --device-id nrf9160-pendant --role nrf_pendant

	  The relay stores only a SHA-256 of the secret, so the token is
	  printed exactly once and is unrecoverable afterwards. It carries the
	  nrf_pendant scope set and nothing else: announce, audio upload,
	  speech read, event write, mac:plan, mac:jobs:read, transcribe,
	  heartbeat-self. It cannot reach /v1/ops/*, cannot declare routines,
	  cannot execute on the Mac, and cannot act as another device. If the
	  pendant is lost, revoke this one token and nothing else changes:

	    pendant-credentials.mjs revoke --token-id <tokenId>

	  When set, this takes precedence over PENDANT_RELAY_API_KEY on every
	  relay request. Keep it out of source control: put it in secrets.conf,
	  which is gitignored, and remember that a built image embeds it in
	  plaintext (build*/zephyr/.config, autoconf.h, zephyr.elf, zephyr.bin).

config PENDANT_MIC_INJECT
	bool "Debug: replace captured mic audio with debugger-injected PCM"
	default n
	help
	  Build a debug-only audio injection path. When armed over SWD, every
	  512-sample mic stage is overwritten with PCM the host streams into a
	  RAM ring buffer before it reaches the Opus encoder, so an end-to-end
	  test carries a byte-identical utterance at a known level instead of
	  whatever the physical microphone happened to hear.

	  This exists because the acoustic test rig (play speech out of a
	  laptop speaker at the pendant) has two uncontrolled variables -
	  speaker distance and host output volume - which make level-sensitive
	  conclusions unfalsifiable. Digital injection removes both.

	  Costs ~4.1 KB of RAM for the ring and its control block, and leaves
	  the microphone hardware running but ignored. Leave this off in
	  production: it is a harness feature, not a product feature.

config PENDANT_OFFLINE_STORE
	bool "Offline store-and-forward outbox on microSD"
	default y
	help
	  The one capability no other node in this system can have: the
	  pendant is worn and holds non-volatile storage, so it can be
	  present and remember while the link is down.

	  Adds a durable FIFO outbox on the microSD card holding two kinds of
	  item: a voice memo whose upload failed, and a moment bookmark the
	  owner dropped without speaking. Items are delivered oldest-first
	  when the link returns and are removed ONLY after the relay confirms
	  receipt.

	  This never writes an extra byte of audio. record_microphone()
	  already journals to SD only when there is no live uplink at press
	  time; this option only decides whether that journal is kept
	  (upload failed) or discarded (upload succeeded).

	  Costs one manifest struct in RAM (see PENDANT_OFFLINE_STORE_SLOTS)
	  and no buffers: audio is streamed straight off the card.

config PENDANT_OFFLINE_STORE_SLOTS
	int "Outbox depth"
	depends on PENDANT_OFFLINE_STORE
	default 4
	range 1 8
	help
	  Queue slots. Each costs 48 B of RAM and, for a voice item, one
	  file on the card. Four is about an afternoon of dead-zone captures
	  at the 30 s recording cap; raise it only with the RAM budget in
	  hand (the build has ~8.9 kB free).

config PENDANT_ALERT_INBOX
	bool "Hold relay alerts on the device until the owner interacts"
	depends on PENDANT_OFFLINE_STORE
	default y
	help
	  Alerts the relay raised while the owner was unreachable are pulled
	  when there is signal, written to the microSD card, and surfaced on
	  the owner's next button press — which is exactly the moment the
	  link may be gone again. Reading them needs no radio at all.

	  The acknowledgement rides back out through the outbox rather than
	  the radio, so "the owner read this" survives being offline too.

	  Reuses the shared HTTP response buffer, so it owns no RAM beyond
	  the alert cursor inside the outbox manifest.

config PENDANT_ALERT_STATE_PATH
	string "Relay path the alert inbox polls"
	depends on PENDANT_ALERT_INBOX
	default "/v1/state/pendant-alerts"
	help
	  A device-scoped persistent-state key. The owner's Mac, a routine or
	  the dashboard PUTs {"data":{"alerts":[{"id":…,"text":…}]}} here with
	  the admin credential; the pendant may only read it.
	  /v1/announcements would be the natural source but is admin-scoped —
	  a paired device gets 403.

	  Reading it needs scope pendant:alerts:read, which the nrf_pendant
	  role holds. NOT state:read: that one scope covers the whole /v1/state
	  key space, including the Mac's agent-snapshot and fleet world-model,
	  and a device that can be lost should not be able to read those. The
	  relay grants the narrow scope on THIS EXACT PATH only
	  (cloud-relay/relayScopes.js), so changing this string means changing
	  that rule too or the poll starts taking a 403.

config PENDANT_BOOT_AGENT_JOB_ID
	string "Existing relay job to download at boot for diagnostics"
	default ""
	help
	  When non-empty, download the Mac-generated pendantSpeech payload from
	  this relay job after LTE initialization. Leave empty in production.

config PENDANT_REFLEX_SELFTEST
	bool "Debug: install a reflex test recipe at boot when the card has none"
	default n
	help
	  The microSD card cannot be mounted from the host, so a bare card
	  cannot carry a hand-written recipes.json for bring-up.  With this
	  option, a boot that finds NO recipes.json installs a RAM-only armed
	  10-second countdown recipe (id=1: LED burst + haptic double + chime)
	  exercising every reflex action kind, including the full I2S chime
	  path, without any file on the card.

	  RAM-only is the safety property: the test recipe is never written to
	  the card, cannot masquerade as an owner-installed recipe, and stops
	  appearing the moment a real recipes.json exists.  It costs no RAM
	  beyond the recipe table slot it borrows.  Leave off in production
	  builds; enable via reflex-selftest.conf for hardware verification.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
